From: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] rm: do not free blocks for inodes that have no block pointers

delete_file() walks i_block[] unconditionally and hands every word to
ext2fs_block_alloc_stats(..., -1). For a fast symlink that array is not
block pointers at all -- it holds the target path inline -- so the target's
own bytes are freed as if they were blocks.

Deleting a symlink to /lib/systemd/system/serial-getty@.service frees
i_block[10], the trailing "e\0\0\0" of ".service", as block 101. Words that
land out of range print "Illegal block number" and are ignored; one that
lands in range is silently cleared in the block bitmap. The next file
written to the image is then handed a block that filesystem metadata still
owns, and the kernel remounts the filesystem read-only on first mount.

debugfs, which this code is derived from, guards the same call with
ext2fs_inode_has_valid_blocks2(). Restore that guard. It also covers
device nodes, FIFOs, sockets and inline-data inodes, which have no block
pointers either.

Reproducer, on a fresh image:

    mke2fs -q -t ext4 -F t.img 16384
    debugfs -w -R "symlink /link /lib/systemd/system/serial-getty@.service" t.img
    e2rm t.img:/link
    fsck.ext4 -fn t.img          # Block bitmap differences: +101

Signed-off-by: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/src/util.c b/src/util.c
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/src/util.c
+++ b/src/util.c
@@ -206,12 +206,15 @@ delete_file(ext2_filsys fs, ext2_ino_t inode)
       return(retval);
     }

-  if ((retval = ext2fs_block_iterate(fs, inode, 0, NULL,
-                                     release_blocks_proc, NULL)))
+  /* i_block[] holds the target path, not block pointers, for a fast symlink;
+     the same is true of device nodes, FIFOs, sockets and inline data. */
+  if (ext2fs_inode_has_valid_blocks2(fs, &inode_buf) &&
+      (retval = ext2fs_block_iterate(fs, inode, 0, NULL,
+                                     release_blocks_proc, NULL)))
     {
       fprintf(stderr, "%s\n", error_message(retval));
       return(retval);
     }

   ext2fs_inode_alloc_stats(fs, inode, -1);

   return(0);
